| Option                                  | Real PySpark?            | Suitability                                    |
| --------------------------------------- | ------------------------ | ---------------------------------------------- |
| Browser-only Pyodide                    | No full JVM-backed Spark | Not suitable                                   |
| DuckDB/Polars pretending to be PySpark  | No                       | Incorrect semantics and APIs                   |
| Local-mode Spark in isolated containers | Yes                      | Best initial architecture                      |
| Shared Spark Connect server             | Yes, with API limits     | Useful later for controlled trusted workloads  |
| Spark on Kubernetes                     | Yes, distributed         | Best for advanced scaling                      |
| Databricks compute                      | Yes                      | Optional specialized integration, not required |

### Why local-mode Spark is the right starting point

Apache Spark explicitly supports local execution using `local[2]`, `local[4]`, and similar configurations. These execute real Spark jobs using local CPU cores.&#x20;

[image](https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=https://spark.apache.org\&sz=32)

Spark 4.0.1 Documentation



For our first 300 or so ordinary DataFrame challenges, depending on their actual content, local-mode Spark should be sufficient.

It supports real `SparkSession`, DataFrames, SQL, joins, aggregations, windows, Parquet, execution plans, and RDD operations.

But local mode does not simulate a genuine multi-node network. For lessons about cross-node behavior or production cluster performance, we will eventually need distributed Spark workers.

### Why I wouldn't start with a shared Spark Connect server

Spark Connect is attractive because it separates clients from Spark servers, but it does not support `SparkContext` and RDD APIs. Our curriculum explicitly includes both. More importantly, Spark itself treats arbitrary user-code execution as a fundamental capability, not something it sandboxes automatically.&#x20;

[image](https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=https://spark.apache.org\&sz=32)

Spark 4.2.0 Documentation

+1



So my choice is:

Start with isolated classic PySpark jobs. Add distributed Spark on Kubernetes for advanced labs. Keep Spark Connect as an optional future execution adapter, not our only engine.

## 3. How Run and Submit will actually work

Imagine a learner solving Q0127 — Accounts Without Transactions.

They write:

```
def solve(accounts, transactions):    return accounts.join(        transactions,        on="account_id",        how="left_anti"    )
```

When the learner clicks Run, the system follows this sequence:

01

Receive code

Frontend sends question ID, code and execution mode to the API

02

Create execution job

API checks limits, identifies the dataset fixture and queues the job

03

Start isolated environment

Runner launches a sandbox containing real PySpark and the permitted dataset files

04

Load input DataFrames

Trusted harness creates accounts and transactions using the required schemas

05

Execute learner code

Spark evaluates the returned DataFrame and materializes a bounded preview

06

Return results

Frontend receives output rows, schema, errors and execution status

The same infrastructure handles Submit, but with a different dataset fixture and grading process.

| Behavior                    | Run                            | Submit                    |
| --------------------------- | ------------------------------ | ------------------------- |
| Dataset                     | Visible sample                 | Private test fixtures     |
| Output                      | Rows, schema, execution errors | Passed/failed results     |
| Full expected answer        | Visible for provided sample    | Hidden                    |
| Saves progress              | Can save code                  | Records submission result |
| Typical resource allocation | Small                          | Depends on challenge      |
| Objective                   | Experiment and debug           | Verify correctness        |

On Submit, the system should recreate a clean environment for each independent test fixture. The submitted code must not access reference solutions or expected outputs.

