## 7. How real streaming and Delta projects will execute

Advanced exercises need a more capable runner.

Take P09, our clickstream project.

The learner implements Structured Streaming logic that processes event batches, performs event-time aggregations, handles late events, and writes outputs.

Our testing system can supply reproducible batches:

P09 — Streaming test scenario

| Batch 1    | Product views and clicks                    |
| ---------- | ------------------------------------------- |
| Batch 2    | Add-to-cart and purchase events             |
| Batch 3    | Delayed events with earlier timestamps      |
| Restart    | Resume from an isolated checkpoint          |
| Validation | Verify final aggregates and replay behavior |

The engine would:

1. Create an isolated project workspace.
2. Inject a known sequence of streaming files or events.
3. Execute the learner's Structured Streaming query.
4. Stop and restart the job for recovery tests.
5. Inspect output tables and checkpoint-dependent behavior.
6. Return correctness and diagnostic feedback.

This can initially use file-based streaming sources, which Spark officially supports, so we do not need a dedicated Kafka cluster for the first streaming exercises.&#x20;

[image](https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=https://spark.apache.org\&sz=32)

Spark 4.2.0-preview5 Documentation



For Delta Lake projects, each execution gets a private Delta table directory and transaction log. That enables realistic `MERGE`, SCD Type 2, schema evolution, and time-travel tests without corrupting another learner's work.

## 8. Performance labs need a different environment

This is where we should be careful.

A two-core local Spark worker is perfectly reasonable for learning joins, windows, and explain plans. But it cannot accurately represent an enterprise cluster with multiple physical machines.

I recommend three execution tiers:

| Tier            | Workload                                                | Engine                              |
| --------------- | ------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------- |
| A — Standard    | Lessons, most practice, Playground                      | Isolated local-mode Spark           |
| B — Engineering | Delta, ETL, streaming, small performance tests          | Isolated higher-resource Spark jobs |
| C — Distributed | Cluster tuning, true network shuffle, failure scenarios | Kubernetes-backed Spark cluster     |

We don't need Tier C at launch, provided advanced lessons requiring it aren't advertised as fully executable until their runtime is ready.

Apache Spark supports launching applications on Kubernetes with separate driver and executor pods, so there is a documented scaling route when we need it.&#x20;

[image](https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=https://spark.apache.org\&sz=32)

