## 9. Security is a release blocker, not an optional enhancement

There is one fundamental issue: learners are writing arbitrary Python code, and Apache Spark will execute that code.

A regular Docker container alone is not a sufficient security boundary for running hostile multi-tenant workloads. We should use hardened workload isolation such as gVisor or microVMs, plus strict restrictions and security testing.&#x20;

[image](https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=https://github.com\&sz=32)

GitHub

+1



Our public execution system must enforce:

- Separate sandbox per execution or securely isolated learner workspace.
- CPU, memory, process-count, disk, output-size, and wall-clock limits.
- No outbound internet access for ordinary exercises; only necessary internal Spark communication.
- Read-only dataset mounts and separate writable scratch storage.
- No root privileges, privileged containers, host mounts, Docker socket, or cloud credentials exposed to learner code.
- Execution cancellation that terminates the complete process tree and job resources.
- API rate limits, per-user concurrency limits, and queue quotas.
- Trusted grading logic and expected answers outside learner-accessible files.

For example, these are reasonable initial experimental limits, to tune after measurement:

| Resource                    | Starter policy                          |
| --------------------------- | --------------------------------------- |
| CPU                         | 2 vCPU                                  |
| Memory                      | 4 GiB                                   |
| Ordinary Run timeout        | 45 seconds                              |
| Submit timeout              | 90 seconds total, subject to test count |
| Maximum displayed rows      | 100                                     |
| Concurrent jobs per learner | 1                                       |
| Project/streaming limits    | Separate controlled budget              |

Those are our proposed limits, not proven capacity figures. Spark startup time, Python worker processes, memory usage, and fixture size will determine the real limits.

## 10. Deployment architecture for runtime.school

Since the learning frontend can stay on Vercel, we should split frontend delivery from execution infrastructure.

Vercel — runtime.school frontend

Next.js pages, editor, dataset browser, progress, authentication interface

Backend service — execution API

FastAPI, request validation, job orchestration, progress/status endpoints

Private job infrastructure

Queue, sandbox scheduler, PySpark worker images, grading orchestration

Persistent storage

PostgreSQL for metadata, object storage for datasets and project artifacts

Render is an option for hosting the API and ordinary background services because it supports Docker deployments and background workers. But we must separately verify a host capable of launching strongly isolated per-user sandboxes; a normal Render background worker is not automatically a secure code-execution platform.&#x20;

