## 11. API and codebase design

I would introduce a separate execution service in the runtime.school repository.

```
runtime-school/
  apps/
    web/                     # Existing Next.js frontend

  services/
    spark-execution/
      api/
        jobs.py
        submissions.py
        results.py
      scheduler/
        queue.py
        quotas.py
        cancellation.py
      runner/
        harness.py
        dataset_loader.py
        output_serializer.py
      grading/
        dataframe.py
        pipeline.py
        streaming.py
        performance.py
      adapters/
        local_spark.py
        kubernetes_spark.py

  infrastructure/
    spark-images/
    sandbox-policies/

  curriculum/
    pyspark/
      datasets/
      lessons/
      questions/
      projects/
```

The frontend calls a simple contract:

```
POST /api/spark/jobs
```

```
{
  "mode": "run",
  "question_id": "Q0127",
  "code": "def solve(accounts, transactions):\n    ...",
  "dataset_version": "1.0.0"
}
```

The API returns a job ID and initial status. The frontend subscribes to status updates or polls for completion rather than keeping a long-running HTTP request open.

The server must resolve the dataset and execution policy from trusted question metadata—not simply trust any fixture path, image, or resource request supplied by the browser.

## 12. How we should build it, in order

01

Local PySpark proof of execution

One pinned Spark image, one dataset, one learner function, structured output and errors

A real join executes with no fake PySpark APIs

02

Sandbox and execution API

FastAPI, job queue, isolation, cancellation, CPU/memory limits and abuse controls

Untrusted code cannot access host or other learner resources

03

Correctness grader

Visible tests, hidden tests, schema checks, duplicate-sensitive comparisons, feedback

Valid solutions pass; flawed solutions fail predictably

04

Connect the learning tools

Playground, Dataset Explorer, Spark SQL Lab, Compare Approaches and explain plans

All tools use the same dataset versions and execution contracts

05

Projects and stateful workloads

Multi-file projects, isolated Delta tables, streaming fixtures, restart/replay tests

Project milestones are reproducible and independently gradable

06

Production scaling

Warm sandbox capacity, queue monitoring, user quotas and distributed Spark for advanced labs

Concurrent jobs remain isolated, responsive and within budget

We should also test startup latency and cost before deciding how many sandboxes to keep warm. Spark takes more resources to initialize than ordinary Python execution, so the user experience should communicate when a job is queued, starting, running, or complete.

## Final recommendation

I would build one reusable Spark Execution Platform, initially backed by real PySpark running in isolated local-mode environments.

That same platform will power our 90 lessons, 450 practice questions, 10 projects, and six tools using the 15 shared datasets.

We should avoid creating a custom Spark interpreter, a shared unrestricted Spark server, or an expensive Databricks dependency at the beginning.

The first concrete engineering milestone should be: open a PySpark lesson in runtime.school, write a real DataFrame transformation, click Run, see the actual Spark output, click Submit, and receive correct feedback from hidden tests—all running in a safely isolated environment.

Once that complete flow works with D01 Retail and D02 Banking, we have the foundation to build the rest of PySpark without redesigning the engine every time.